why 1 0 SPACING The piece opens with the bass on a deep E, two octaves under the tenor, before it moves up an octave on the next beat.
why 3 3/4 DOUBLING With the bass moving G–C within one C chord, the doubled E is soprano and alto sharing the chord's third for a moment.
why 3 3/4 SIX_FOUR The bass leaps G–C under one C major chord, so the ⁶₄ is just the first half of an arpeggio; the chord is heard in root position a moment later.
why 3 7/8 LEAPS The bass steps F♯–G and leaps a fourth to C. The tritone F♯–C spans two chords.
why 4 1/4 LEAPS After its leap down to F♯ the bass keeps falling E–D before turning to G. It descends through the chord instead of turning back.
why 5 1/2 MELODIC The bass leaps down a diminished seventh, C to D♯, to put the leading tone in the bass for V⁶. An expressive leap, settled by the step D♯–E that follows.
why 8 1/2 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Soprano and tenor fall B–A and E–D a fifth apart, the soprano an eighth earlier. Staggered like this Bach accepts them; strict exercises still count them.
why 8 3/4 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence on G (III): soprano A–G, the alto's F♯ drops to D, the seventh C to B and the bass to G. All the resolutions point down.
why 10 0 SIX_FOUR At the fermata the alto holds G over the D chord as a suspension and falls G–F♯–E. The ⁶₄ is only the suspension's moment; the chord is D major.
why 11 3/8 UNRESOLVED_NCT With the bass on B, the chord is B minor with an added seventh (B–D–F♯–A), so the bass's B is a chord tone. The bass leaps through it on the way to E.
why 11 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices step down into C major (VI⁶), the bass by a fifth. Each line falls by its own interval.
why 13 3/8 LEAPS The bass steps D–E and skips up to G♯, the leading tone of A minor. The tritone D–G♯ spans two chords.
why 13 1/2 LEADING_TONE The bass resolves G♯ to A, but an octave lower: a drop of a seventh instead of a step up. The leading tone reaches its note; the bass just changes register for a deep A minor chord.
why 13 1/2 MELODIC The bass leaps down a major seventh, G♯ to A, instead of stepping up to A. The same resolution an octave lower, which gives the chord a deep root.
why 15 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION The piece ends on E major, a Picardy third. Every voice falls into it: the alto's D♯ drops to B, the seventh A to G♯ and the bass to a deep E.
